      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Human rhinoviruses (HRVs) are a common cause of upper respiratory tract infections. There is growing evidence that HRVs are also important in lower respiratory tract infections and often induce asthma exacerbations."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "We evaluated the presence of HRV in the lower respiratory tract by obtaining bronchial biopsies from infants with recurrent asthmalike respiratory symptoms."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "A total of 201 steroid-naive infants age 3 to 26 months with recurrent respiratory symptoms for at least 4 weeks within the preceding 2 months were studied for lung function using body plethysmography. Bronchoscopy was performed in 68 children, and bronchial biopsies were available from 59 infants for HRV detection with in situ hybridization."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Human rhinovirus was detected in 21 of 47 (45%) specimens. Abnormal lung function (decreased airways conductance) was found in 18 of 21 (86%) HRV(+) infants and in 15 of 26 (58%) HRV(-) infants (P = .037). Occurrence of a respiratory infection in the 6 weeks preceding bronchoscopy correlated with HRV positivity (P = .036)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Human rhinovirus is frequently found in the lower airways in infants with recurrent respiratory symptoms, and the majority of these HRV(+) infants also showed increased airway resistance."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CLINICAL IMPLICATIONS",
          "content": "Human rhinovirus is a common pathogen causing upper and lower respiratory symptoms. Follow-up of these infants will reveal whether the presence of HRV in the bronchial biopsy and abnormal lung function with recurrent respiratory symptoms predicts subsequent asthma."
        }
      ]},
